function e(e){return e.filter(e=>!!e.marker).map(e=>{let t=e.text.indexOf(`. `),n=t>0?e.text.slice(0,t+1):e.text,r=n.length>60?`${n.slice(0,60).replace(/[\s,;:]+$/,``)}…`:n;return{key:e.marker,text:r}})}var t={id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-mp`,title:`Poverty is a story about us, not them`,paragraphs:[{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-mp-A`,marker:`A`,text:`Too often still, we think we know what poverty looks like. It's the way we've been taught, the images we've been force-fed for decades. The chronically homeless. The undocumented immigrant. The urban poor, usually personified as a woman of color, the "welfare queen" politicians still too often reference.`},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-mp-B`,marker:`B`,text:`But as income inequality rises to record levels in the United States, even in the midst of a record economic expansion, those familiar images are outdated, hurtful, and counterproductive to focusing attention on solutions and building ladders of opportunity.`},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-mp-C`,marker:`C`,text:`Today's faces of income inequality and lack of opportunity look like all of us. It's Anna Landre, a disabled Georgetown University student fighting to keep health benefits that allow her the freedom to live her life. It's Tiffanie Standard, a counselor for young women of color in Philadelphia who want to be tech entrepreneurs—but who must work multiple jobs to stay afloat. It's Ken Outlaw, a welder in rural North Carolina whose dream of going back to school at a local community college was dashed by Hurricane Florence—just one of the extreme weather events that have tipped the balance for struggling Americans across the nation.`},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-mp-D`,marker:`D`,text:`If these are the central characters of our story about poverty, what layers of perceptions, myths, and realities must we unearth to find meaningful solutions and support? In pursuit of revealing this complicated reality, Mothering Justice, led by women of color, went last year to the state capital in Lansing, Michigan, to lobby on issues that affect working mothers. One of the Mothering Justice organizers went to the office of a state representative to talk about the lack of affordable childcare—the vestiges (痕迹) of a system that expected mothers to stay home with their children while their husbands worked. A legislative staffer dismissed the activist's concerns, telling her "my husband took care of that—I stayed home. "`},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-mp-E`,marker:`E`,text:`That comment, says Mothering Justice director Danielle Atkinson, "was meant to shame" and relied on the familiar notion that a woman of color concerned about income inequality and programs that promote mobility must by definition be a single mom, probably with multiple kids. In this case, the Mothering Justice activist happened to be married. And in most cases in the America of 2019, the images that come to mind when we hear the words poverty or income inequality fail miserably in reflecting a complicated reality; poverty touches virtually all of us. The face of income inequality, for all but a very few of us, is the one we each see in the mirror.`},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-mp-F`,marker:`F`,text:`How many of us are poor in the U. S. ? It depends on who you ask. According to the Census Bureau, 38 million people in the U. S. are living below the official poverty thresholds. Taking into account economic need beyond that absolute measure, the Institute for Policy Studies found that 140 million people are poor or low-income. That's almost half the U. S. population.`},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-mp-G`,marker:`G`,text:`Whatever the measure, within that massive group, poverty is extremely diverse. We know that some people are more affected than others, like children, the elderly, people with disabilities, and people of color.`},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-mp-H`,marker:`H`,text:`But the fact that 4 in 10 Americans can't come up with $ 400 in an emergency is a commonly cited statistic for good reason; economic instability stretches across race, gender, and geography. It even reaches into the middle classes, as real wages have stagnated (不增长) for all but the very wealthy and temporary spells of financial instability are not uncommon.`},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-mp-I`,marker:`I`,text:`Negative images remain of who is living in poverty as well as what is needed to move out of it. The big American myth is that you can pull yourself up by your own efforts and change a bad situation into a good one. The reality is that finding opportunity without help from families, friends, schools, and community is virtually impossible. And the playing field is nothing close to level.`},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-mp-J`,marker:`J`,text:`The FrameWorks Institute, a research group that focuses on public framing of issues, has studied what sustains stereotypes and narratives of poverty in the United Kingdom. "People view economic success and wellbeing in life as a product of choice, willpower, and drive," says Nat Kendall-Taylor, CEO of FrameWorks. "When we see people who are struggling," he says, those assumptions "lead us to the perception that people in poverty are lazy, they don't care, and they haven't made the right decisions. "`},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-mp-K`,marker:`K`,text:`Does this sound familiar? Similar ideas surround poverty in the U. S. And these assumptions give a false picture of reality. "When people enter into that pattern of thinking," says Kendall-Taylor, "it's cognitively comfortable to make sense of issues of poverty in that way. It creates a kind of cognitive blindness—all of the factors external to a person's drive and choices that they've made become invisible and fade from view."`},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-mp-L`,marker:`L`,text:`Those external factors include the difficulties accompanying low-wage work or structural discrimination based on race, gender, or ability. Assumptions get worse when people who are poor use government benefits to help them survive. There is a great tension between "the poor" and those who are receiving what has become a dirty word; "welfare. "`},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-mp-M`,marker:`M`,text:`According to the General Social Survey, 71 percent of respondents believe the country is spending too little on "assistance to the poor." On the other hand, 22 percent think we are spending too little on "welfare"; 37 percent believe we are spending too much.`},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-mp-N`,marker:`N`,text:`"Poverty has been interchangeable with people of color—specifically black women and black mothers," says Atkinson of Mothering Justice. It's true that black mothers are more affected by poverty than many other groups, yet they are disproportionately the face of poverty. For example, Americans routinely overestimate the share of black recipients of public assistance programs.`},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-mp-O`,marker:`O`,text:`In reality, most people will experience some form of financial hardship at some point in their lives. Indeed, people tend to dip in and out of poverty, perhaps due to unexpected obstacles like losing a job, or when hours of a low-wage job fluctuate.`},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-mp-P`,marker:`P`,text:`Something each of us can do is to treat each other with the dignity and sympathy that is deserved and to understand deeply that the issue of poverty touches all of us.`}]},n={"cet4-2020-12-s2":{subtitle:`2020 年 12 月 · 第 2 套`,minutes:125,words:1400,tags:[`真题`,`四级`],summary:`2020年12月英语四级第2套真题，写作考交通方式的变化，翻译考春节团圆饭，长篇阅读探讨贫困与我们每个人息息相关，仔细阅读涉及无聊的创造力与西方国家森林增长，听力给出原文并由站内合成朗读。`,parts:[{id:`writing`,kind:`writing`,title:`Part I 写作`,instructions:`Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write on the topic Changes in the Way of Transportation. You should write at least 120 words but no more than 180 words.`,minutes:30,reference:{essay:`In recent decades, China has witnessed remarkable changes in the way of transportation. From bicycles to private cars and high-speed rails, the means of transport have evolved significantly. The shift from bicycles to cars and high-speed trains reflects not only economic development but also technological progress. The widespread use of private vehicles has made travel more convenient and comfortable. Moreover, high-speed rail networks have revolutionized long-distance travel, reducing travel times and increasing efficiency. For example, students can return home for a weekend without spending an entire day on the journey. In many cities, buses and subways also give residents more choices for daily travel. In conclusion, the changes in transportation have greatly facilitated people's lives. Looking forward, it is anticipated that the development of greener and smarter transportation systems will contribute to sustainable development.`,guidance:[`开篇点题：In recent decades, China has witnessed remarkable changes in the way of transportation 直接回应"交通方式的变化"这一命题，From bicycles to private cars and high-speed rails 用具体交通工具的演进勾勒变化的主线。`,`主体前半（变化与意义）：The shift from bicycles to cars and high-speed trains reflects not only economic development but also technological progress 以 not only…but also 点明变化背后反映的经济发展与技术进步；The widespread use of private vehicles has made travel more convenient and comfortable 写私家车带来的便利舒适。`,`主体后半（高铁与日常出行）：high-speed rail networks have revolutionized long-distance travel, reducing travel times and increasing efficiency 突出高铁缩短时间、提升效率；For example, students can return home for a weekend 以学生周末返乡为例佐证，buses and subways also give residents more choices 再补日常公共交通的多样化。`,`结尾总结与展望：In conclusion, the changes in transportation have greatly facilitated people's lives 总结变化极大便利了生活；Looking forward, it is anticipated that the development of greener and smarter transportation systems will contribute to sustainable development 展望更绿色、更智能的交通促进可持续发展。全文约 160 词，符合 120–180 词要求。`]},questions:[{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-w1`,number:1,type:`writing`,stem:`For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write on the topic Changes in the Way of Transportation. You should write at least 120 words but no more than 180 words.`,answer:`见参考`,explanation:`命题作文：以"交通方式的变化"为题，须写出变化的具体表现与意义。参考范文开篇总起中国交通的显著变化；主体以自行车→私家车→高铁的演进为主线，用 not only…but also 点明经济与技术进步的支撑，并以学生周末返乡、公交地铁为例；结尾 In conclusion 总结变化便利生活，并展望绿色智能交通。结构为"总起—例证—总结展望"，词数 120–180。`,score:15}]},{id:`listening`,kind:`listening`,title:`Part II 听力理解`,instructions:`Directions: In this section, you will hear two long conversations. At the end of each conversation, you will hear four questions. Both the conversation and the questions will be spoken only once. After you hear a question, you must choose the best answer from the four choices marked A), B), C), and D). Then mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 1 with a single line through the centre. Directions: In this section, you will hear two passages. At the end of each passage, you will hear three or four questions. Both the passage and the questions will be spoken only once. After you hear a question, you must choose the best answer from the four choices marked A), B), C), and D). Then mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 1 with a single line through the centre. Directions: In this section you will hear three recordings of lectures or talks followed by three or four questions. The recordings will be played only once. After you hear a question, you must choose the best answer from the four choices marked A), B), C) and D). Then mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 1 with a single line through the centre.`,minutes:25,audio:{note:`站内合成朗读（非考试原音）`,segments:[{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-ls-1`,label:`News Report 1`,questionNo:1,text:`A waiter has returned a cheque worth nearly $424,000 to a retired social worker who lost it. The waiter found a bank envelope while cleaning off a table last Saturday at a restaurant. He ran outside, but the customer was gone. He opened the envelope and got a shock. After an unsuccessful search, the restaurant's owner called the Daily News for help. The relieved customer was reunited with her cheque on Wednesday. It contained money from her apartment sale, already planned for a down payment on a new home. The customer did not tip the waiter after her meal. She tried to give him money later on, but he graciously declined. The waiter, who's working his way through school, did accept the customer's apology and gratitude and said he was happy to have helped her.`},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-ls-2`,label:`News Report 2`,questionNo:3,text:`The village of Marif in Alaska voted on Tuesday to move to the state's mainland. The move is due to global warming and rising sea levels. Most of the village's 169 registered voters took part in the town hall meeting. They decided in a vote of 89 to 78 to move from their land on Saracef Island near the Arctic Circle. Marif Council Secretary Donna Barr said the vote was largely symbolic. It will be costly financially to the community. About 15 years ago, they estimated the cost at $180 million. I would figure it's much higher now, Barr said. We don't see the move happening in our lifetime because of the funding. The village's roughly 650 residents have seen warming temperatures, melt sea ice, and permanently frozen land. This has resulted in houses falling into the water. At least 31 villages in Alaska face immediate threats due to climate change, the Government Accountability Office reported in 2009.`},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-ls-3`,label:`News Report 3`,questionNo:5,text:`A man in Halifax, Canada, wanted to find out if people were thankful for someone holding the door open for them. The social experiment showed that 99 out of 100 people expressed gratitude. I didn't think we were going to get 99. I don't know why, but I was pleasantly surprised because it went beyond just thank you. People got into conversations with us, said Steve Foran, CEO of Gratitude at Work. What we know from research is that from grateful people come good things, he said. A simple way to induce gratitude in people is opening doors, and so we went to six places and opened the doors for people. For the experiment, Foran's team went to a shopping centre, a mall, two office buildings, and a coffee shop. The door was held for 15 to 20 people at a time at each location. We did have one that didn't say thank you. We're not here to judge them, because on any given day, that could be me or you. I suspect out of the 100 people, there were probably a bunch of them having a bad day. But grateful people make people grateful, said Foran.`},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-ls-4`,label:`Conversation 1`,questionNo:8,text:`Rays Solar, Lisa speaking. How can I help? Hi, my name is Winston. I wish to inquire about solar panel installations. Yes, what would you like to know? Well, my neighbor installed panels on his roof about a year ago in order to power his hot water. He tells me it has saved him over $500 thus far. Does that sound about right to you? Well, I'm not familiar with your neighbor or his particular setup, but that amount is definitely possible. I can tell you that the average four-bedroom house may typically have a roof with 50 square meters of surface area. Four panels on one side of that roof could save a family of four around $300 a year. Okay, that sounds about right then. My house is about the size you described, but my neighbor's is bigger. I'm not sure how many panels he has up there, but he does have a large family of six. Are you interested in installing some solar panels on your roof, sir? Yes, I'm considering it. If you wish to come into our office, we could show you the different solutions we offer. Okay, I might do that. But just quickly, if you don't mind, could you tell me approximately how much a typical installation costs? Like, say, four panels? Prices do vary depending on different factors, but as a rough estimate, it's around $2,000. But you know, a typical household will make back that initial investment in about five years. Okay, I see. Thank you.`},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-ls-5`,label:`Conversation 2`,questionNo:12,text:`Good afternoon. Sorry to have kept you waiting. How can I help you? Oh, no problem. I'm interested in booking a holiday to Australia and wonder if you could tell me what deals you have. Sure. Are you only looking for flights or a package holiday with everything included? When you say everything, what do you mean? Well, a package holiday would include your flights, hotels, meals, day trips to different places of interest, and transport to and from the airport. Yeah, that sounds pretty good. I'm going with my family, so it would be nice to have everything taken care of. So, what sort of deals do you have for package holidays, then? All sorts, really. How long do you want to go for and on what dates? Two weeks around Christmastime would be great. Okay, let me check that for you. Here's one. Fourteen nights in southeastern Australia, five nights in Sydney, and five nights in Melbourne. And then for the other four nights, you can choose from a list of trips to other places nearby. You could visit Canberra, for example, or the Blue Mountains, or you could go for a drive down the Great Ocean Road. Also, if you're interested in wine, you could go on a tour of the places where they grow grapes and make wine. That sounds great. It's good we can choose some activities ourselves.`},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-ls-6`,label:`Passage 1`,questionNo:16,text:`Tourists taking a holiday in the Indonesian island of Bali are facing a new $14 per person tax when they arrive on the holiday island from next year. But this is a green tax, which Bali Governor Wayan Koster has been working on for months and which is designed to help clean up the island's natural environment. And with good reason, too. Indonesia is drowning in plastics. Recycling is not one of the country's strong points. It's not uncommon to be offered many more plastic bags than one could ever need when visiting supermarkets and shopping malls. But slowly, things are starting to change for the better. Back in 2016, the medium-sized city of Banjarmesin banned single-use plastic bags. The city of Bogor followed suit in 2018. A few months ago, Koster announced a plan that would not only ban single-use plastic bags from supermarkets and convenience stores, but plastic bags and straws across the island. The regulation will come into full effect next month. We received a fast and quick response from the Balinese people. Not only positive responses from the Balinese, we received good responses from the central government, other local governments, and even from overseas, Koster told the Sydney Morning Herald this week during an interview. The governor is a determined environmentalist, and he has more laws planned to protect the island's waterways, in particular, and to support the introduction of electric vehicles, too.`},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-ls-7`,label:`Passage 2`,questionNo:19,text:`An endangered species of whale is experiencing a small baby boom off the coast of America. The North Atlantic right whale is one of the rarest species of whale on the planet, numbering only about 411. But the Centre for Coastal Studies said Friday that its aerial survey team spotted a mum with two babies in Cape Cod Bay a day earlier. That brings the number seen in nearby waters alone this year to three. That's big news because the whale population has been falling, and no baby whales were seen last year. In all, seven baby whales have been spotted so far this year. The whale population has become endangered due to commercial whaling activities in recent years. This is because they are sometimes hunted for their meat or their skin. Over-hunting could lead to the disappearance of the whale population, possibly causing major problems to the global food chain. The whales give birth off the southeast coast of America in the winter and travel to feeding grounds off the northeast coast in the early spring. The northeast coast is a critically important source of food. The animals often feed close to shore. This provides watchers on land with unbeatable views of one of the rarest of marine mammals. It's illegal to get within 1,500 feet of the animals without a federal research permit, so whale watchers are discouraged from attempting to get close to the whales.`},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-ls-8`,label:`Passage 3`,questionNo:22,text:`An average person consumes 144 pints of milk a year, but 40% of that is poured onto cereal, and 60% of those people are children. But what was once advertised as nutritious is becoming unpopular. Americans drink 37% less milk than they did in the 1970s, and in the UK, dairy consumption overall has fallen by a third in the past 20 years. Milk is increasingly being described in a negative light. A recent blog suggested, maybe people are drinking less milk because it is poisonous to many of us. Lactose is the sugar found in milk and dairy products. It needs a series of complex proteins to break it down. Without enough of these proteins, the lactose is broken down by bacteria in the human body. This can cause physical pain and produce gas in the stomach. However, after we have finished breast or formula feeding, most of us don't continue producing the complex proteins in our body, which are necessary to break down the lactose. Despite the problems in digesting milk, it does provide many benefits. Milk is nutritious. It contains vitamins A and D, as well as protein, and isn't full of calories. You can test yourself by drinking a large glass of milk. If you get sick in your stomach within the next 24 hours, you are lacking the proteins to digest milk.`}]},questions:[{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-l1`,number:1,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-ls-1`,stem:`What does the news report say about the waiter?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) He wanted to buy a home.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) He suffered from a shock.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) He lost a huge sum of money.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) He did an unusual good deed.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`原文 "A waiter has returned a cheque worth nearly $424,000 to a retired social worker who lost it"——服务员把近 42.4 万美元的支票物归原主，且拒收酬金，是不同寻常的善举，选 D；B 只是发现信封时吃了一惊，并非报道主旨。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-ls-1`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-l2`,number:2,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-ls-1`,stem:`What did the customer try to do when she got her cheque back?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Invite the waiter to a fancy dinner.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Tell her story to the Daily News.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Give some money to the waiter.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Pay the waiter's school tuition.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`原文 "She tried to give him money later on, but he graciously declined"——顾客拿回支票后想给服务员钱以示感谢，被婉拒，选 C。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-ls-1`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-l3`,number:3,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-ls-2`,stem:`What is Marif's vote on Tuesday about?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Whether or not to move to the state's mainland.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) How to keep the village from sinking into the sea.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Where to get the funds for rebuilding their village.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) What to do about the rising level of the seawater.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`原文 "The village of Marif in Alaska voted on Tuesday to move to the state's mainland"——周二投票表决的是村庄是否迁往该州大陆，选 A。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-ls-2`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-l4`,number:4,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-ls-2`,stem:`Why did Donna Barr say they wouldn't see the plan carried out any time soon?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It takes too long a time.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It costs too much money.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It has to wait for the state's final approval.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It faces strong opposition from many villagers.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`原文 "It will be costly financially to the community… $180 million… We don't see the move happening in our lifetime because of the funding"——搬迁耗资巨大、资金无着落，选 B。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-ls-2`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-l5`,number:5,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-ls-3`,stem:`What is the purpose of the social experiment?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) To investigate whether people are grateful for help.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) To see whether people hold doors open for strangers.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) To explore ways of inducing gratitude in people.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) To find out how people express gratitude.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`原文 "wanted to find out if people were thankful for someone holding the door open for them"——实验目的是调查人们对他人帮助是否心怀感激，选 A。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-ls-3`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-l6`,number:6,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-ls-3`,stem:`What did Steve Foran and his team do in the experiment?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) They induced strangers to talk with them.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) They helped 15 to 20 people in a bad mood.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) They held doors open for people at various places.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) They interviewed people who didn't say thank you.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`原文 "we went to six places and opened the doors for people… a shopping centre, a mall, two office buildings, and a coffee shop"——团队在多个场所为陌生人开门，选 C。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-ls-3`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-l7`,number:7,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-ls-3`,stem:`What do we learn from the news report?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) People can be educated to be grateful.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Most people express gratitude for help.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Most people have bad days now and then.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) People are ungrateful when in a bad mood.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`原文 "The social experiment showed that 99 out of 100 people expressed gratitude"——绝大多数人都会对帮助表达感激，选 B。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-ls-3`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-l8`,number:8,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-ls-4`,stem:`What is the man's purpose for calling the woman?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) To order a solar panel installation.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) To report a serious leak in his roof.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) To enquire about solar panel installations.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) To complain about the faulty solar panels.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`原文 "I wish to inquire about solar panel installations"——男士打电话是咨询太阳能板安装事宜，选 C；他并未下订单，故 A 错。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-ls-4`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-l9`,number:9,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-ls-4`,stem:`What do we learn about the man's neighbor from the conversation?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) He plans to install solar panels.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) He owns a four-bedroom house.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) He saves $300 a year.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) He has a large family.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`原文 "he does have a large family of six"——邻居是个六口之家，选 D；C 的每年省 300 美元是四口之家的估算，并非邻居的情况。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-ls-4`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-l10`,number:10,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-ls-4`,stem:`What is one of the man's chief concerns?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) The service of the solar panel company.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) The cost of a solar panel installation.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) The maintenance of the solar panels.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) The quality of the solar panels.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`原文 "could you tell me approximately how much a typical installation costs?"——男士主要关心安装费用，选 B。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-ls-4`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-l11`,number:11,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-ls-4`,stem:`How long will it take a typical household to make back the initial investment?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) One year and a half.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Less than four years.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Roughly six years.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) About five years.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`原文 "a typical household will make back that initial investment in about five years"——一般家庭约五年收回初期投资，选 D。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-ls-4`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-l12`,number:12,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-ls-5`,stem:`Where is the conversation taking place?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) At a travel agency.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) At an Australian airport.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) At an airline transfer service.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) At a local transportation authority.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`对话中工作人员说 "I'm interested in booking a holiday… what deals you have" 并介绍 package holiday，可见发生在旅行社，选 A。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-ls-5`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-l13`,number:13,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-ls-5`,stem:`Why is the woman interested in package holidays?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) She would be able to visit more scenic spots.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) She wanted to save as much money as possible.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) She would like to have everything taken care of.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) She wanted to spend more time with her family.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`原文 "I'm going with my family, so it would be nice to have everything taken care of"——女士想要一切都被安排妥当，选 C。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-ls-5`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-l14`,number:14,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-ls-5`,stem:`How long does the woman want to go for the holiday?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Four days.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Five days.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) One week.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Two weeks.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`原文 "Two weeks around Christmastime would be great"——女士想度假两周，选 D。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-ls-5`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-l15`,number:15,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-ls-5`,stem:`What does the woman say she likes about the holiday package?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Choosing some activities herself.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Spending Christmas with Australians.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Driving along the Great Ocean Road.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Learning more about wine making.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`原文 "It's good we can choose some activities ourselves"——女士喜欢可以自己选择一些活动，选 A。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-ls-5`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-l16`,number:16,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-ls-6`,stem:`What will tourists have to do when they visit Indonesia's Bali island?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Bring their own bags when shopping.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Use public transport when traveling.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Dispose of their trash properly.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Pay a green tax upon arrival.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`原文 "facing a new $14 per person tax when they arrive… this is a green tax"——游客抵达巴厘岛时须缴纳绿色税，选 D。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-ls-6`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-l17`,number:17,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-ls-6`,stem:`What does the passage say about Indonesia?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It has not been doing a good job in recycling.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It has witnessed a rise in accidental drowning.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It has not attracted many tourists in recent years.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It has experienced an overall decline in air quality.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`原文 "Indonesia is drowning in plastics. Recycling is not one of the country's strong points"——印尼回收工作做得不好，选 A；drowning in plastics 是"被塑料淹没"的比喻，并非溺水事件增加。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-ls-6`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-l18`,number:18,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-ls-6`,stem:`What is the new plan Governor Koster recently announced?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) To charge a small fee on plastic products in supermarkets.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) To ban single-use plastic bags and straws on Bali Island.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) To promote the use of paper bags for shopping.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) To impose a penalty on anyone caught littering.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`原文 "a plan that would not only ban single-use plastic bags… but plastic bags and straws across the island"——新计划在全岛禁用塑料袋和吸管，选 B。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-ls-6`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-l19`,number:19,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-ls-7`,stem:`What do we learn from the passage about the North Atlantic right whale?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It gives birth to several babies at a time.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It is the least protected mammal species.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Its breeding grounds are now better preserved.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Its population is now showing signs of increase.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`原文 "experiencing a small baby boom… seven baby whales have been spotted so far this year"——北大西洋露脊鲸数量出现回升迹象，选 D。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-ls-7`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-l20`,number:20,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-ls-7`,stem:`What has caused the decline of the whale population in recent years?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Global warming.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Polluted seawaters.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Commercial hunting.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Decreasing birthrates.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`原文 "The whale population has become endangered due to commercial whaling activities in recent years"——商业捕鲸导致其濒危、数量下降，选 C。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-ls-7`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-l21`,number:21,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-ls-7`,stem:`Why do the whales travel to the northeast coast of America in the early spring?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) To mate.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) To look for food.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) To escape hunters.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) To seek breeding grounds.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`原文 "travel to feeding grounds off the northeast coast in the early spring. The northeast coast is a critically important source of food"——early spring 前往东北海岸是为觅食，选 B；交配/产仔在东南海岸的冬季。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-ls-7`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-l22`,number:22,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-ls-8`,stem:`What does the passage say about Americans?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) They prefer to drink low-fat milk.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) They think milk is good for health.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) They consume less milk these days.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) They buy more milk than the British.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`原文 "Americans drink 37% less milk than they did in the 1970s"——美国人现在喝的牛奶比上世纪七十年代少 37%，选 C。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-ls-8`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-l23`,number:23,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-ls-8`,stem:`How do Americans and British people think of milk nowadays?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It is not as healthy as once thought.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It is not easy to stay fresh for long.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It benefits the elderly more.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It tends to make people fat.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`原文 "Milk is increasingly being described in a negative light… maybe people are drinking less milk because it is poisonous to many of us"——如今牛奶被认为不如从前健康，选 A。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-ls-8`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-l24`,number:24,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-ls-8`,stem:`Why does drinking milk cause pain in some people?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) They drink too many pints every day.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) They are sensitive to certain minerals.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) They lack the necessary proteins to digest it.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) They have eaten food incompatible with milk.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`原文 "Without enough of these proteins, the lactose is broken down by bacteria… This can cause physical pain"——缺乏分解乳糖的复杂蛋白质导致腹痛，选 C。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-ls-8`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-l25`,number:25,type:`single`,groupId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-ls-8`,stem:`What does the passage say is a benefit of milk?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It is easier for sick people to digest.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It provides some necessary nutrients.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It is healthier than other animal products.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It supplies the body with enough calories.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`原文 "Milk is nutritious. It contains vitamins A and D, as well as protein, and isn't full of calories"——牛奶提供维生素与蛋白质等必要营养，选 B；D 与 isn't full of calories 相悖。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-ls-8`},score:1}]},{id:`reading`,kind:`reading`,title:`Part III 阅读理解`,instructions:`Directions: In this section, there is a passage with ten blanks. You are required to select one word for each blank from a list of choices given in a word bank following the passage. Read the passage through carefully before making your choices. Each choice in the bank is identified by a letter. Please mark the corresponding letter for each item on Answer Sheet 2 with a single line through the centre. You may not use any of the words in the bank more than once. Directions: In this section. you are going to read a passage with ten statements attached to it. Each statement contains information given in one of the paragraphs. Identify the paragraph from which the information is derived. You may choose a paragraph more than once. Each paragraph is marked with a letter. Answer the questions by marking the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 2. Directions: There are 2 passages in this section. Each passage is followed by some questions or unfinished statements. For each of them there are four choices marked A), B), C), and D). You should decide on the best choice and mark the corresponding letter on Answer Sheet 2 with a single line through the centre.`,minutes:40,bank:{A:`choose`,B:`constant`,C:`disappointing`,D:`distinguish`,E:`exhausting`,F:`experienced`,G:`negative`,H:`outcome`,I:`pattern`,J:`plural`,K:`repeatedly`,L:`rewarded`,M:`separately`,N:`simply`,O:`undertaken`},passages:[{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-cp`,paragraphs:[{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-cp-1`,text:`When my son completes a task, I can't help but praise him. It's only natural to give praise where praise is due, right? But is there such a thing as too much praise?`},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-cp-2`,text:`According to psychologist Katherine Phillip, children don't benefit from __26__ praise as much as we'd like to think. "Parents' often praise, believing they are building their child's self-confidence. However, over-praising can have a __27__ effect," says Phillip. "When we use the same praise __28__, it may become empty and no longer valued by the child. It can also become an expectation that anything they do must be __29__ with praise. This may lead to the child avoiding taking risks due to fear of __30__ their parents. "`},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-cp-3`,text:`Does this mean we should do away with all the praise? Phillip says no. "The key to healthy praise is to focus on the process rather than the __31__. It is the recognition of a child's attempt, or the process in which they achieved something, that is essential," she says. "Parents should encourage their child to take the risks needed to learn and grow. "`},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-cp-4`,text:`So how do we break the __32__ of praise we're all so accustomed to? Phillip says it's important to __33__ between "person praise" and "process praise". "Person praise is __34__ saying how great someone is. It's a form of personal approval. Process praise is acknowledgement of the efforts the person has just __35__ Children who receive person praise are more likely to feel shame after losing," says Phillip.`}]},t,{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-p1`,paragraphs:[{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-p1-1`,text:`Boredom has, paradoxically, become quite interesting to academics lately. In early May, London's Boring Conference celebrated seven years of delighting in dullness. At this event, people flocked to talk about weather, traffic jams, and vending-machine sounds, among other sleep-inducing topics.`},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-p1-2`,text:`What, exactly, is everybody studying? One widely accepted psychological definition of boredom is "the distasteful experience of wanting, but being unable, to engage in satisfying activity." But how can you quantify a person's boredom level and compare it with someone else's? In 1986, psychologists introduced the Boredom Proneness Scale, designed to measure an individual's overall tendency to feel bored. By contrast, the Multidimensional State Boredom Scale, developed in 2008, measures a person's feelings of boredom in a given situation.`},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-p1-3`,text:`Boredom has been linked to behavior issues including inattentive driving, mindless snacking, excessive drinking, and addictive gambling. In fact, many of us would choose pain over boredom. One team of psychologists discovered that two-thirds of men and a quarter of women would rather self-administer electric shocks than sit alone with their thoughts for 15 minutes. Researching this phenomenon, another team asked volunteers to watch boring, sad, or neutral films, during which they could self-administer electric shocks. The bored volunteers shocked themselves more and harder than the sad or neutral ones did.`},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-p1-4`,text:`But boredom isn't all bad. By encouraging self-reflection and daydreaming, it can spur creativity. An early study gave participants abundant time to complete problem-solving and word-association exercises. Once all the obvious answers were exhausted, participants gave more and more inventive answers to combat boredom. A British study took these findings one step further* asking subjects to complete a creative challenge (coming up with a list of alternative uses for a household item). One group of subjects did a boring activity first, while the others went straight to the creative task. Those whose boredom pumps had been primed were more productive.`},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-p1-5`,text:`In our always-connected world, boredom may be a hard-to-define state, but it is a fertile one. Watch paint dry or water boil, or at least put away your smartphone for a while, and you might unlock your next big idea.`}]},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-p2`,paragraphs:[{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-p2-1`,text:`Forests in countries like Brazil and the Congo get a lot of attention from environmentalists, and it is easy to see why. South America and sub-Saharan Africa are experiencing deforestation on an enormous scale: every year almost 5 million hectares are lost. But forests are also changing in rich Western countries. They are growing larger, both in the sense that they occupy more land and that the trees in them are bigger. What is going on?`},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-p2-2`,text:`Forests are spreading in almost all Western countries, with the fastest growth in places that historically had rather few trees. In 1990 28% of Spain was forested; now the proportion is 37%. In both Greece and Italy, the growth was from 26% to 32% over the same period. Forests are gradually taking more land in America and Australia. Perhaps most astonishing is the trend in Ireland. Roughly 1% of that country was forested when it became independent in 1922. Now forests cover 11% of the land, and the government wants to push the proportion to 18% by the 2040s.`},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-p2-3`,text:`Two things are fertilising this growth. The first is the abandonment of farmland, especially in high, dry places where nothing grows terribly well. When farmers give up trying to earn a living from farming or herding, trees simply move in. The second is government policy and subsidy. Throughout history, governments have protected and promoted forests for diverse reasons, ranging from the need for wooden warships to a desire to promote suburban house-building. Nowadays forests are increasingly welcome because they suck in carbon pollution from the air. The justifications change; the desire for more trees remains constant.`},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-p2-4`,text:`The greening of the West does not delight everyone. Farmers complain that land is being taken out of use by generously subsidised tree plantations. Parts of Spain and Portugal suffer from terrible forest fires. Others simply dislike the appearance of forests planted in neat rows. They will have to get used to the trees, however. The growth of Western forests seems almost as unstoppable as deforestation elsewhere.`}]}],questions:[{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-c26`,number:26,type:`bank`,stem:`According to psychologist Katherine Phillip, children don't benefit from __26__ praise as much as we'd like to think.`,answer:`B`,explanation:`constant 持续的、不停的：children don't benefit from constant praise 指孩子并不从"不绝于耳的"表扬中获益那么多，与下文 over-praising、the same praise repeatedly 呼应；修饰 praise 需形容词，选 B。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-cp-2`,quote:`children don't benefit from __26__ praise as much as we'd like to think.`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-c27`,number:27,type:`bank`,stem:`However, over-praising can have a __27__ effect, says Phillip.`,answer:`G`,explanation:`negative 负面的：over-praising can have a negative effect 意为"过度表扬会产生负面效果"，与前文"表扬有益"形成转折；修饰 effect 需形容词，选 G。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-cp-2`,quote:`over-praising can have a __27__ effect`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-c28`,number:28,type:`bank`,stem:`When we use the same praise __28__, it may become empty and no longer valued by the child.`,answer:`K`,explanation:`repeatedly 反复地：use the same praise repeatedly 指"反复使用同样的表扬"，这样表扬会变得空洞、不再被孩子珍视；修饰动词 use 需副词，选 K。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-cp-2`,quote:`When we use the same praise __28__, it may become empty`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-c29`,number:29,type:`bank`,stem:`It can also become an expectation that anything they do must be __29__ with praise.`,answer:`L`,explanation:`rewarded 得到回报的：be rewarded with praise 为固定搭配，指"所做一切都必须得到表扬作为回报"；must be 后需过去分词构成被动，选 L。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-cp-2`,quote:`anything they do must be __29__ with praise`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-c30`,number:30,type:`bank`,stem:`This may lead to the child avoiding taking risks due to fear of __30__ their parents.`,answer:`C`,explanation:`disappointing 使失望：fear of disappointing their parents 指"害怕让父母失望"，因而回避冒险；介词 of 后需动名词，选 C。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-cp-2`,quote:`fear of __30__ their parents`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-c31`,number:31,type:`bank`,stem:`The key to healthy praise is to focus on the process rather than the __31__.`,answer:`H`,explanation:`outcome 结果：focus on the process rather than the outcome 指"关注过程而非结果"，outcome 与 process 形成对照，且冠词 the 后需名词，选 H。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-cp-3`,quote:`focus on the process rather than the __31__`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-c32`,number:32,type:`bank`,stem:`So how do we break the __32__ of praise we're all so accustomed to?`,answer:`I`,explanation:`pattern 模式：break the pattern of praise 指"打破我们习以为常的表扬模式"，we're all so accustomed to 为定语从句修饰 pattern；the 后需名词，选 I。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-cp-4`,quote:`break the __32__ of praise we're all so accustomed to`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-c33`,number:33,type:`bank`,stem:`Phillip says it's important to __33__ between "person praise" and "process praise".`,answer:`D`,explanation:`distinguish 区分：distinguish between A and B 为固定搭配，指"区分'对人的表扬'与'对过程的表扬'"；it's important to 后接动词原形，选 D。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-cp-4`,quote:`it's important to __33__ between "person praise" and "process praise"`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-c34`,number:34,type:`bank`,stem:`Person praise is __34__ saying how great someone is.`,answer:`N`,explanation:`simply 仅仅、只不过：Person praise is simply saying how great someone is 指"对人的表扬仅仅是说某人有多棒"；修饰动名词 saying 需副词，选 N。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-cp-4`,quote:`Person praise is __34__ saying how great someone is.`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-c35`,number:35,type:`bank`,stem:`Process praise is acknowledgement of the efforts the person has just __35__.`,answer:`O`,explanation:`undertaken 承担、付出（undertake 的过去分词）：the efforts the person has just undertaken 指"这个人刚刚付出的努力"；has just 后需过去分词，选 O。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-cp-4`,quote:`the efforts the person has just __35__`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-m36`,number:36,type:`single`,stem:`One legislative staffer assumed that a woman of color who advocated affordable childcare must be a single mother.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`E`,explanation:`E 段 "relied on the familiar notion that a woman of color concerned about income inequality and programs that promote mobility must by definition be a single mom, probably with multiple kids"——立法助理认为这位有色人种女性一定是单身母亲，选 E。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-mp-E`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-m37`,number:37,type:`single`,stem:`People from different races, genders, and regions all suffer from a lack of financial security.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`H`,explanation:`H 段 "economic instability stretches across race, gender, and geography"——经济不稳定跨越种族、性别和地域，即各群体都缺乏经济保障，选 H。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-mp-H`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-m38`,number:38,type:`single`,stem:`According to a survey, while the majority believe too little assistance is given to the poor, more than a third believe too much is spent on welfare.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`M`,explanation:`M 段 "71 percent… spending too little on 'assistance to the poor'… 22 percent… too little on 'welfare'; 37 percent believe we are spending too much"——多数人认为对穷人救助不足，逾三分之一认为福利开支过多，选 M。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-mp-M`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-m39`,number:39,type:`single`,stem:`A research group has found that Americans who are struggling are thought to be lazy and to have made the wrong decisions.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`J`,explanation:`J 段 FrameWorks Institute 的研究指出，那些"leading to the perception that people in poverty are lazy, they don't care, and they haven't made the right decisions"——奋斗中的人被看作懒惰、决策失误，选 J。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-mp-J`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-m40`,number:40,type:`single`,stem:`Under the old system in America, a mother was supposed to stay home and take care of her children.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`D`,explanation:`D 段 "the vestiges (痕迹) of a system that expected mothers to stay home with their children while their husbands worked"——旧制度期望母亲居家育儿、丈夫外出工作，选 D。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-mp-D`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-m41`,number:41,type:`single`,stem:`It was found that nearly 50% of Americans are poor or receive low pay.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`F`,explanation:`F 段 "140 million people are poor or low-income. That's almost half the U. S. population"——1.4 亿人贫困或低收入，接近美国人口一半，选 F。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-mp-F`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-m42`,number:42,type:`single`,stem:`Americans usually overestimate the number of blacks receiving welfare benefits.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`N`,explanation:`N 段 "Americans routinely overestimate the share of black recipients of public assistance programs"——美国人通常高估黑人领取公共援助的比例，选 N。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-mp-N`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-m43`,number:43,type:`single`,stem:`It is impossible for Americans to lift themselves out of poverty entirely on their own.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`I`,explanation:`I 段 "finding opportunity without help from families, friends, schools, and community is virtually impossible"——没有家人、朋友、学校和社区的帮助，几乎不可能摆脱贫困，选 I。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-mp-I`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-m44`,number:44,type:`single`,stem:`Nowadays, it seems none of us can get away from income inequality.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`E`,explanation:`E 段 "poverty touches virtually all of us. The face of income inequality, for all but a very few of us, is the one we each see in the mirror"——贫困与收入不平等触及我们每个人，选 E。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-mp-E`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-m45`,number:45,type:`single`,stem:`Assumptions about poor people become even more negative when they live on welfare.`,options:e(t.paragraphs),answer:`L`,explanation:`L 段 "Assumptions get worse when people who are poor use government benefits to help them survive… 'welfare'"——穷人靠福利为生时，对他们的成见更负面，选 L。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-mp-L`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-r46`,number:46,type:`single`,stem:`When are people likely to experience boredom, according to an accepted psychological definition?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) When they don't have the chance to do what they want.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) When they don't enjoy the materials they are studying.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) When they experience something unpleasant.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) When they engage in some routine activities.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`第二段公认定义 "the distasteful experience of wanting, but being unable, to engage in satisfying activity"——想参与却无法参与满意的活动，即没有机会做想做的事，选 A。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-p1-2`,quote:`the distasteful experience of wanting, but being unable, to engage in satisfying activity.`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-r47`,number:47,type:`single`,stem:`What does the author say boredom can lead to?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Determination.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Concentration.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Mental deterioration.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Harmful conduct.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`第三段 "Boredom has been linked to behavior issues including inattentive driving, mindless snacking, excessive drinking, and addictive gambling"——无聊与一系列有害行为相关，选 D；C 的心智退化未提及。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-p1-3`,quote:`Boredom has been linked to behavior issues including inattentive driving, mindless snacking, excessive drinking, and addictive gambling.`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-r48`,number:48,type:`single`,stem:`What is the finding of one team of psychologists in their experiment?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Volunteers prefer watching a boring movie to sitting alone deliberating.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Many volunteers choose to hurt themselves rather than endure boredom.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Male volunteers are more immune to the effects of boredom than females.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Many volunteers are unable to resist boredom longer than fifteen minutes.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`第三段 "two-thirds of men and a quarter of women would rather self-administer electric shocks than sit alone with their thoughts for 15 minutes"——许多志愿者宁可电击自己也不愿忍受无聊，选 B。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-p1-3`,quote:`two-thirds of men and a quarter of women would rather self-administer electric shocks than sit alone with their thoughts for 15 minutes.`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-r49`,number:49,type:`single`,stem:`Why does the author say boredom isn't all bad?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) It stimulates memorization.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It allows time for relaxation.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) It may promote creative thinking.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) It may facilitate independent learning.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`第四段 "By encouraging self-reflection and daydreaming, it can spur creativity"——无聊通过自我反思和白日梦激发创造力，选 C。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-p1-4`,quote:`By encouraging self-reflection and daydreaming, it can spur creativity.`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-r50`,number:50,type:`single`,stem:`What does the author suggest one do when faced with a challenging problem?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Stop idling and think big.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Unlock one's smartphone.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Look around oneself for stimulation.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Allow oneself some time to be bored.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`末段 "Watch paint dry or water boil, or at least put away your smartphone for a while, and you might unlock your next big idea"——建议放下手机、给自己一段无聊的时间以激发灵感，选 D。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-p1-5`,quote:`Watch paint dry or water boil, or at least put away your smartphone for a while, and you might unlock your next big idea.`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-r51`,number:51,type:`single`,stem:`What is catching environmentalists' attention nowadays?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Rich countries are stripping poor ones of their resources.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Forests are fast shrinking in many developing countries.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Forests are eating away the fertile farmland worldwide.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Rich countries are doing little to address deforestation.`}],answer:`B`,explanation:`第一段 "South America and sub-Saharan Africa are experiencing deforestation on an enormous scale: every year almost 5 million hectares are lost"——许多发展中国家森林正快速消失，选 B。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-p2-1`,quote:`South America and sub-Saharan Africa are experiencing deforestation on an enormous scale: every year almost 5 million hectares are lost.`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-r52`,number:52,type:`single`,stem:`Which countries have the fastest forest growth?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Those that have newly achieved independence.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Those that have the greatest demand for timber.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Those that used to have the lowest forest coverage.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Those that provide enormous government subsidies.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`第二段 "with the fastest growth in places that historically had rather few trees"——过去树木最少的地区森林增长最快，选 C。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-p2-2`,quote:`with the fastest growth in places that historically had rather few trees.`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-r53`,number:53,type:`single`,stem:`What has encouraged forest growth historically?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) The government's advocacy.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) The use of wood for fuel.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) The favourable climate.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) The green movement.`}],answer:`A`,explanation:`第三段 "Throughout history, governments have protected and promoted forests for diverse reasons"——历史上政府出于各种理由保护、推广森林，选 A。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-p2-3`,quote:`Throughout history, governments have protected and promoted forests for diverse reasons`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-r54`,number:54,type:`single`,stem:`What accounts for our increasing desire for forests?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Their unique scenic beauty.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) Their use as fruit plantations.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Their capability of improving air quality.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Their stable supply of building materials.`}],answer:`C`,explanation:`第三段 "Nowadays forests are increasingly welcome because they suck in carbon pollution from the air"——森林越来越受欢迎是因为能吸收空气中的碳污染、改善空气质量，选 C。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-p2-3`,quote:`Nowadays forests are increasingly welcome because they suck in carbon pollution from the air.`},score:1},{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-r55`,number:55,type:`single`,stem:`What does the author conclude about the prospects of forestation?`,options:[{key:`A`,text:`A) Deserts in sub-Saharan Africa will diminish gradually.`},{key:`B`,text:`B) It will play a more and more important role in people's lives.`},{key:`C`,text:`C) Forest destruction in the developing world will quickly slow down.`},{key:`D`,text:`D) Developed and developing countries are moving in opposite directions.`}],answer:`D`,explanation:`末段 "The growth of Western forests seems almost as unstoppable as deforestation elsewhere"——西方森林增长与其他地区森林砍伐同样不可阻挡，二者方向相反，选 D。`,evidence:{paragraphId:`cet4-2020-12-s2-p2-4`,quote:`The growth of Western forests seems almost as unstoppable as deforestation elsewhere.`},score:1}]},{id:`translation`,kind:`translation`,title:`Part IV 翻译`,instructions:`Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to translate a passage from Chinese into English. You should write your answer on Answer Sheet 2.`,minutes:30,reference:{translation:`Eating a reunion dinner on the eve of the Spring Festival is a tradition among Chinese people. The reunion dinner is the most important meal of the year and the best time for family reunions, especially for families whose members live in different places. The dishes at the reunion dinner are rich and varied, some of which have special meanings. For instance, fish is an indispensable dish because the Chinese character for 'fish' sounds the same as the character for 'surplus'. In many parts of China, dumplings are also an important delicacy, as they symbolize wealth and good fortune.`},questions:[{id:`cet4-2020-12-s2-t1`,number:1,type:`translation`,stem:`春节前夕吃团圆饭是中国人的传统。团圆饭是一年中最重要的晚餐，也是家庭团聚的最佳时机，家人生活在不同地方的家庭尤其如此。团圆饭上的菜肴丰富多样，其中有些菜肴有特殊含义。例如，鱼是不可缺少的一道菜，因为汉语中的"鱼"字和"余"字听上去一样。在中国的许多地方，饺子也是一道重要的佳肴，因为饺子象征着财富和好运。`,answer:`见参考`,explanation:`要点：①"春节前夕吃团圆饭"译为 Eating a reunion dinner on the eve of the Spring Festival，动名词作主语；"是中国人的传统"用 is a tradition among Chinese people。②"一年中最重要的晚餐，也是家庭团聚的最佳时机"处理为 the most important meal of the year and the best time for family reunions；"家人生活在不同地方的家庭尤其如此"用 especially for families whose members live in different places，whose 引导定语从句。③"菜肴丰富多样，其中有些菜肴有特殊含义"译为 The dishes… are rich and varied, some of which have special meanings，some of which 引导非限制性定语从句。④"鱼是不可缺少的一道菜"用 fish is an indispensable dish；"'鱼'字和'余'字听上去一样"译为 the Chinese character for 'fish' sounds the same as the character for 'surplus'。⑤末句"饺子也是一道重要的佳肴"dumplings are also an important delicacy，"象征着财富和好运"用 as they symbolize wealth and good fortune 表原因；全文以一般现在时为主，注意长句切分与逻辑衔接。`,score:15}]}]}};export{n as default};